A||N||12||rigdonhome||EGR Tube Failure on '88 2.8L V6||||6||
Z||000000||rigdonhome||09-03-2001||12:42 PM||||OK, I know that this is a common failure...  The only fix I've seen is to just replace with another factory tube...  The problem is, the factory setup is a poor design (I have some experience in this type of component design...) because it bolts directly from the exhaust to the intake.  The intake and exhaust will vibrate at distinctly different frequencies, and since the tube goes straight between the two it becomes a structural member that is not strong enough to take this type of load.  There should have been a 'jog' or a 'loop' designed into this tube from day 1 similar to that which you find in brake lines just before the master cylinder.  The purpose of this is to soak up vibration and allow for shifts in distance between the two mount points caused by expansion/contraction of different materials.<P>So I hesitate to spend $100 on a new tube that is just going to break like the old one.  I would like to replace the middle of the tube with a flexible line or maybe a piece of soft copper tube with a loop in it.  The problem is that I don't know what to use that will stand up to the extreme heat.  I don't think copper will work...<P>So has anyone else 'redesigned' their EGR tube to eliminate this problem?  If so, what did you do???<P>Help!?!  I have the Fiero apart and need to drive it to work tomorrow!!!<P>Cheers,<BR>Scott<P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/ezra_driving_signature.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/ezra_rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||6
Z||000001||Formula88||09-03-2001||12:46 PM||||I saw a flexible braided steel EGR hose at the Nationals that was being sold by FOCOA, but good luck getting anything from them in a timely manner.  It was about $65 at the show.  I think the regular price is around $75-79.  I've also heard of other sources for a braided steel EGR, but I don't have them with me.  Perhaps someone will post them?  I need a replacement for my 88 as well.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rigdonhome||09-03-2001||08:20 PM||||Thanks!<P>I'll look into it...  I am planning my own flexible versoin, but would probably buy the braided one for looks and long-term function...  Tonight I took the old tube out of the flanges and threaded in a 3/8" pipe thread nipple...  The flange holes were already exactly the right size!  I plan to seal the threads with JB-Weld...  This leaves me with the option of just about any type of flexible plumbing available to fit between the two flanges/nipples.  I am hoping I can find a piece of braided hose for a natural gas stove?  I'll post my fix after I hit Lowe's tomorrow... <P>Still looking for other ideas, though!<P>Cheers,<BR>Scott<P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/ezra_driving_signature.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/ezra_rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000003||skitime||09-03-2001||09:49 PM||||I have repaired EGR tubes by brazing the cracks.  The brazing material can take the temprature that is found on the tube. I believe therefore the copper tube would too. Let me know how you make out.<P><P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||rigdonhome||09-04-2001||06:35 AM||||I looked at the Fiero Store and at FOCOA and all I could find was a stock replacement...  <P>I went to Lowe's and bought soft copper 3/8" ID tubing as well as two (2) brass fittings that go from 3/8 copper compression on one end to 3/8 pipe thread on the other.  The 3/8" pipe thread fits directly in the original EGR tube flange holes.  I plan to seal the threads/connections with J.B. Weld to help with strength and vacuum sealing.  <P>As far as I can tell no one else has done this before.  I'll take pictures and offer them to anyone interested.  I'll also let you know how long it lasts!!!  It should be stronger than the original setup and will also have a loop to soak up the vibration.<P>I would still be thrilled to see any sources for that braided steel flexible tube!!!  ???<P>Cheers,<BR>Scott<P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/ezra_driving_signature.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/ezra_rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000005||rigdonhome||09-04-2001||07:08 AM||||I found a source for the braided hose!!!<P>The company doesn't seem to have a web site, but I found a dude on the net that has their catalog scanned in on his web site:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Lane/1588/quickcat.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Lane/1588/quickcat.html</A> <P>The source for the hose is IRM:<P>International Research Motorsports, Inc.<BR>18100 Cashell Rd.<BR>Rockville, MD 20853<P>Order Desk: 800-367-8423<BR>Technical Product & Shipping Information: 301-948-3301<P>Below (should be!) an image of the ad:<BR> <A HREF="http://24.249.68.73/cars/pennocks/fiero_flex_egr_tube.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://24.249.68.73/cars/pennocks/fiero_flex_egr_tube.jpg</A> <P>I'll probably do the copper tube thing and see how it works before I spend $90 on the flex tube...  If my copper deal looks like crap I'll spend the $90 just for looks.  Never know, though, the copper might look nice as long as I do a good job with the install...<P>Cheers,<BR>Scott<P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/ezra_driving_signature.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/ezra_rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000006||Formula88||09-04-2001||10:03 AM||||Braided Natural Gas hose sounds like a possibility.  I would think any large home improvement store should have some.  I may check that out this week myself.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rigdonhome||09-06-2001||09:29 PM||||Just wanted to put out the word that I got the IRM stainless steel flex tube in the mail today and it is a superior quality piece compared to the stock one.  It fit perfectly and shouldn't break like the original.<P>Two Thumbs Up...  Highly Recommended!<P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/ezra_driving_signature.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/ezra_rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000008||tesmith66||09-07-2001||05:44 AM||||I used the EGR tube from a 2.8 litre Firebird. I had to elongate the holes on one end, but that was it. It's a little longer than the original, MUCH stronger and only cost $1.00. By far the cheapest fix I have found.<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||14
Z||000009||rigdonhome||09-07-2001||06:41 AM||||NOW you tell me!!!<P>I read in the archives that the Firebird hose would work, but not anything about it being stronger...  What is different about it?  Different attachments at the flanges?  Bigger tube?<P><P>------------------<BR>Scott Rigdon<BR>1988 Fiero GT, Black, 5-speed<BR>38,000 Original Miles!<BR>Web Site:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/</A> <BR>Story of buying my Fiero:   <A HREF="http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.RigdonHome.com/cars/88_Fiero_GT/14_hours_in_a_fiero.htm</A> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/ezra_driving_signature.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/rear_signature.jpg">   <IMG SRC="http://24.249.68.73/cars/88_fiero_gt/ezra_rear_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||12
Z||000010||Mickey_Moose||09-07-2001||10:40 AM||||tube is more beefer...thicker<P>was roaming around the local T/A and Camaro wrecker the other day, and noticed a lot of parts from the T/A will fit the old Fiero, headlights switch (and some models have the grey/black combo vs the all black ones), noticed that the GTA steering wheels are also close (may be too big) as a replacement for the GT wheels...shifter knobs for the automatics, standard - not sure about the thread pitch (but something some can check out)<P>anyways<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000011||GodSend||09-07-2001||11:56 AM||||I have a quick question regarding the firebird tube swap.<P>  I have one sitting at home.  It is not bent on the right angles (ie.... if I attach one side of the tube.. the other end is no where close).  Any suggestions on the easiest method to use to bend this thing into the right shape (it bends by hand.. but I need the right shape)....<P>p.s. - old Egr tube is missing.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||tesmith66||09-07-2001||12:37 PM||||I attached it to the intake plenum first, then to the EGR adapter. I then slid the EGR end down over the studs, loosely attached the valve and nuts, then bolted the plenum down. After that, I tightened the EGR valve down and made any adjustments to the bends by hand.<P>I have had ZERO problems with it since.<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||1
